Instruction:
Write a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text.

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\| Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdownText) {
    // Define a regular expression pattern to match markdown links
    std::regex pattern("\\[(.*?)\\]\\((.*?)\\)");

    // Use the std::regex_replace function to replace markdown links with html links
    std::string htmlText = std::regex_replace(markdownText, pattern, "<a href=\"$2\">$1</a>");

    // Define a regular expression pattern to match inline styles
    std::regex inlineStylePattern("\\{\\s*\\*\\s*\\+\\s*\\{[^}]*?\\s*\\}\\s*");

    // Use the std::regex_replace function to replace inline styles with html attributes
    htmlText = std::regex_replace(htmlText, inlineStylePattern, "<span style=\"$1\">");

    // Define a regular expression pattern to match images
    std::regex imagePattern("\\[.*?\\]\\.(.*?)");

    // Use the std::regex_replace function to replace images with html img tags
    htmlText = std::regex_replace(htmlText, imagePattern, "<img src=\"$2\" alt=\"$1\">");

    return htmlText;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdownText = "[Link Text](https://www.example.com)";
    std::string htmlText = markdownToHtml(markdownText);

    std::cout << "Markdown Text: " << markdownText << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML Text: " << htmlText << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This C++ function uses regular expressions to match markdown links, inline styles, and images in the input string. It then replaces these matches with corresponding html tags, resulting in an output string containing html formatted text. The example usage in the `main` function demonstrates how to use this function to convert markdown text to html text.

